<Window x:Class="pCUE.HelpWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        Title="pCUE - what each control does" Height="620" Width="700"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner" Foreground="#FFE0E5C8" Icon="small.ico">
    <Window.Background>
        <LinearGradientBrush EndPoint="0.5,1" StartPoint="0.5,0">
            <GradientStop Color="Black" Offset="0"/>
            <GradientStop Color="#FF2AE87C" Offset="1"/>
        </LinearGradientBrush>
    </Window.Background>

    <Window.Resources>
        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" x:Key="H">
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
            <Setter Property="FontSize" Value="14"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="White"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,14,0,4"/>
        </Style>
        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" x:Key="P">
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="White"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,6"/>
        </Style>
        <Style TargetType="TextBlock" x:Key="Tip">
            <Setter Property="TextWrapping" Value="Wrap"/>
            <Setter Property="Foreground" Value="Yellow"/>
            <Setter Property="FontWeight" Value="Bold"/>
            <Setter Property="Margin" Value="0,0,0,6"/>
        </Style>
    </Window.Resources>

    <Grid Margin="14">
        <Grid.RowDefinitions>
            <RowDefinition Height="*"/>
            <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>
        </Grid.RowDefinitions>

        <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto" Padding="0,0,10,0">
            <StackPanel>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="CPU box"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    Temperature, clock and load, read through LibreHardwareMonitor.
                    <LineBreak/><Bold>Start</Bold> begins polling. <Bold>Average Values</Bold> swaps the middle
                    column from Min to a running average (the fans have their own Avg column, so this
                    affects the CPU only).
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="Fans box"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    Live speed of each channel, with the minimum, maximum and average since the last
                    <Bold> Reset</Bold>. A channel with no fan, or a fan whose speed-sense wire does not
                    reach the Commander, correctly reads 0.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="Fan controls (the numeric box, slider and mode)"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    Type a number per fan, then press <Bold>Set Speed</Bold>. The box means two things,
                    and the split is deliberate because no real fan runs below 100 RPM:
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>100 or less</Bold> = fan power in <Bold>percent</Bold>.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Over 100</Bold> = a fixed <Bold>RPM</Bold> target.
                    <LineBreak/><Bold>Sync</Bold> makes every fan follow fan #1.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    The drop-down sets how the Commander drives that channel:
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>4-pin</Bold> - PWM fans. Speed is set by a control signal on the
                    fourth wire.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>3-pin</Bold> - DC fans. Speed is set by varying the voltage.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Auto</Bold> - let the Commander decide. It can guess wrong, and a
                    mis-detected channel may ignore your setting entirely.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Disconnect</Bold> - stop driving the channel.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource Tip}">
                    If a fan ignores the percentage, the mode is usually wrong. A PWM fan set to 3-pin
                    often will not spin at all, and a fan left on Auto may run flat out whatever you ask
                    for. Set the mode that matches the fan.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="Fixed RPM, and why it may not work"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    Asking for an RPM makes the <Bold>Commander</Bold> hold that speed itself, by watching
                    the fan's speed-sense wire. That needs two things to be true: the channel must be
                    <Bold>4-pin</Bold>, because the Commander does not regulate by RPM on a DC channel, and
                    the fan's sense wire must actually reach the Commander. If the Fans box shows 0 for a
                    fan that is clearly spinning, it does not - and no RPM target can work. That is what
                    the tachometer below is for.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="Bench Tachometer + closed-loop RPM hold"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    An external USB tachometer supplies the speed reading the Commander is missing.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Connect Tach</Bold> - open the meter.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Fan</Bold> - which fan the meter is pointed at. That fan's live
                    reading then comes from the meter.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Adjust fan speed from Tacho</Bold> - with this ticked, typing an RPM
                    for that fan and pressing <Bold>Set Speed</Bold> makes pCUE hold the speed itself, nudging
                    the fan's power up or down until the meter reads what you asked for. Values of 100 or
                    less are still a plain power percentage.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>Auto connect</Bold> - open the Commander and the meter when pCUE
                    starts. This also stops the iCUE services, exactly as the Open button does.
                    <LineBreak/>• <Bold>BATT LOW</Bold> - replace the meter's battery. A flat battery makes
                    it drift and then stop, which will stall a hold that depends on it.
                </TextBlock>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    The status line shows the loop working: <Bold>Ramping</Bold>, then
                    <Bold>Stabilizing</Bold>, then <Bold>Stable</Bold>. Expect it to settle in under a minute.
                    Fan power moves in whole percent, so the closest reachable speed is usually within
                    20-25 RPM of the target; pCUE stops at the nearest step instead of hunting forever.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="Buttons"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    <Bold>Open</Bold> connects the Commander PRO (and stops the iCUE services, which
                    otherwise fight pCUE for the device). <Bold>Kill iCUE</Bold> does that on its own.
                    <Bold> Reset</Bold> clears the Min/Max/Avg figures. <Bold>Auto Start</Bold> launches pCUE
                    with Windows.
                </TextBlock>

                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource H}" Text="Updates and remote control"/>
                <TextBlock Style="{StaticResource P}">
                    <Bold>Check for Updates</Bold> looks for a newer pCUE. It verifies the download's
                    checksum and always asks before installing; <Bold>On start</Bold> only checks, it never
                    installs on its own.
                    <LineBreak/><Bold>Remote</Bold> lets pCUE be driven over HTTP. With the
                    <Bold> Token</Bold> box empty it listens on this PC only; entering a token is what allows
                    access from other machines, and every such request must then present it.
                    <Bold> Debug log</Bold> records detailed diagnostics to
                    %LOCALAPPDATA%\pCUE\logs, which is what to send if something misbehaves.
                </TextBlock>

            </StackPanel>
        </ScrollViewer>

        <Button Grid.Row="1" Content="Close" HorizontalAlignment="Right" Margin="0,12,0,0" Width="90"
                Height="26" Background="#FF3806FB" Foreground="#FFFBF5F5" IsCancel="True" Click="Close_Click"/>
    </Grid>
</Window>
